CAT = "nzsfeLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("What is the main purpose of the Health Practitioners Competence Assurance Act 2003?",
 "Protecting the public's safety", ["Setting nurses' pay and conditions", "Funding nursing education places", "Running public hospitals"],
 "The Act protects the health and safety of the public by making sure health practitioners are competent and fit to practise their professions."),
("Under the HPCA Act, what must a registered nurse hold, besides registration, to practise?",
 "A current practising certificate", ["A union membership card", "A signed hospital employment contract", "A postgraduate diploma"],
 "A nurse must be registered and hold a current annual practising certificate to practise. Practising without one is not allowed under the Act and can lead to referral to a professional conduct committee."),
("Which body hears disciplinary charges against a nurse under the HPCA Act?",
 "The Disciplinary Tribunal (HPDT)", ["Human Rights Review Tribunal", "Employment Relations Authority", "Health and Disability Commissioner"],
 "A professional conduct committee can refer a matter to the Health Practitioners Disciplinary Tribunal, a separate tribunal that hears charges of professional misconduct. The Commissioner deals with complaints under the Code of Rights."),
("How many rights does the Code of Health and Disability Services Consumers' Rights contain?",
 "10", ["7", "12", "13"],
 "The Code has ten rights, from respect and dignity through to effective communication, informed consent, support, teaching and research, and complaints."),
("Which right in the Code covers access to a competent interpreter?",
 "Right to effective communication", ["Right to be fully informed", "Right to have a support person", "Right to dignity and independence"],
 "Right 5 is effective communication in a form, language and manner the person understands, including a competent interpreter where necessary."),
("Ana asks for her sister to stay with her during an examination. Which right in the Code applies?",
 "Right to support", ["Right to independence", "Right to complain", "Right to services of a standard"],
 "Right 8 gives a consumer the right to have one or more support persons present, unless safety is at risk or another consumer's rights would be unreasonably infringed."),
("What does the Code of Rights presume about every consumer's ability to consent?",
 "Competent unless shown otherwise", ["Only adults over 20 are competent", "A doctor must assess each one first", "Whānau decide for all older adults"],
 "Right 7(2) says every consumer must be presumed competent to make an informed choice and give informed consent unless there are reasonable grounds to believe otherwise."),
("Midway through a dressing change, Aroha says she wants it to stop. What should the nurse do?",
 "Stop and discuss her concerns", ["Finish quickly, then explain", "Ask a second nurse to hold her", "Document it and carry on"],
 "Right 7(7) gives every consumer the right to refuse services and to withdraw consent to services. The nurse stops, explores why and explains any risks."),
("A family is unhappy with their mother's care and asks how to complain. What should the nurse do?",
 "Tell them how to complain", ["Discourage it to protect staff", "Say only doctors take complaints", "Ask them to wait until discharge"],
 "Right 10 gives consumers the right to complain in any appropriate form. Providers must make it easy, and the free Health and Disability Advocacy Service can help."),
("A nurse opens a neighbour's health record out of curiosity, without telling anyone. How is this classed?",
 "A privacy breach", ["Acceptable if nothing is shared", "Allowed for any staff member", "Fine if the neighbour agrees later"],
 "Under the Health Information Privacy Code 2020, health information obtained for one purpose may not be used for another unless a rule 10 exception applies. Looking without a care reason is a breach and a conduct issue."),
("A caller says she is the patient's daughter and asks for his test results. What should the nurse do?",
 "Check the patient agrees first", ["Give results as she is whānau", "Read results out if brief", "Transfer her to the laboratory"],
 "Being whānau does not give a right to health information. The nurse confirms the patient's wishes and identity before disclosing anything."),
("When does an enduring power of attorney for personal care and welfare take effect?",
 "When the person loses capacity", ["As soon as both parties sign it", "When the person turns 65", "When the person is admitted"],
 "Under the Protection of Personal and Property Rights Act 1988, a personal care and welfare EPA comes into effect only when the donor no longer has capacity. A property EPA can instead start straight away."),
("An older man has a valid advance directive refusing CPR that applies to his situation. He has a cardiac arrest. What applies?",
 "Honour the valid directive", ["Start CPR until whānau arrive", "Ask the whānau to decide", "Start CPR, then review it"],
 "Right 7(5) recognises advance directives. A valid directive that applies to the situation is followed, so CPR is not started."),
("Under the End of Life Choice Act 2019, what must a health practitioner not do?",
 "Raise assisted dying first", ["Answer a person's questions", "Refer to palliative care", "Discuss symptom relief"],
 "A health practitioner must not initiate a discussion about assisted dying or suggest it. They may respond when the person raises it."),
("A patient is harmed by a treatment injury in hospital. Which scheme may cover it?",
 "ACC, which is no-fault", ["Pharmac", "The Disciplinary Tribunal", "The hospital's own insurer"],
 "The Accident Compensation Act 2001 covers treatment injury on a no-fault basis, so the person does not need to sue for compensation."),
("A nurse is worried that a child is being abused. Where can she report the concern?",
 "Oranga Tamariki or Police", ["The child's school only", "The Health and Disability Commissioner", "The Nursing Council"],
 "Anyone can make a report of concern to Oranga Tamariki or Police, and Police are called on 111 if the child is in immediate danger. Most services also have a child protection policy and lead."),
("Which death in hospital must be reported to the coroner?",
 "An unexpected death during a procedure", ["An expected death in palliative care", "An expected death from known cancer", "A certified death from known heart disease"],
 "Under the Coroners Act 2006, deaths that are unnatural or violent, without a known cause, or medically unexpected during or as a result of a medical procedure or anaesthetic are reported to the coroner."),
("A former patient sends a nurse a friend request on social media. What is best practice?",
 "Decline and keep boundaries", ["Accept, as care has ended", "Accept but avoid health talk", "Message to ask about recovery"],
 "The Council's guidance on professional boundaries and social media asks nurses not to form personal online relationships with patients or former patients."),
("Which is a sound principle for nursing documentation?",
 "Record care soon after giving it", ["Write notes up at the week's end", "Record personal views on whānau", "Use your own private abbreviations"],
 "Notes are written as soon as practicable after care, are factual and use only approved abbreviations, so they are accurate and stand up to review."),
("A nurse believes a colleague cannot practise safely because of a health condition. What does the HPCA Act require?",
 "Notify the Nursing Council", ["Wait until a patient is harmed", "Tell only the colleague", "Warn other staff by email"],
 "A health practitioner who has reason to believe another practitioner cannot perform their functions because of a mental or physical condition must notify the Nursing Council in writing. Nurses must also tell the Council about their own health condition if it may affect their practice."),
("Under the HPCA Act, who describes the scope of practice for registered nurses?",
 "The Nursing Council", ["Health New Zealand", "The Ministry of Health", "Each employer"],
 "Each responsible authority describes the scopes of practice for its profession. For nurses that is Te Kaunihera Tapuhi o Aotearoa, the Nursing Council of New Zealand."),
("Right 6 of the Code of Rights gives a consumer the right to what?",
 "Be fully informed", ["Choose any clinician", "Free treatment", "Be seen within an hour"],
 "Right 6 covers the information a reasonable consumer would expect: their condition, options with risks and benefits, results and waiting times."),
("An attorney under a personal care and welfare EPA must make a significant decision. What is needed first?",
 "Certified loss of capacity", ["A letter from the whānau", "A signed hospital consent form", "Approval from the charge nurse"],
 "For significant decisions, a health practitioner such as a GP or mental health nurse must have certified that the donor has lost capacity, or the Family Court must have decided it."),
("Under the Care of Children Act 2004, from what age can a young person consent to medical treatment as if an adult?",
 "16", ["14", "18", "20"],
 "Section 36 of the Act gives a young person of 16 or over the same power as an adult to consent to or refuse medical, surgical or dental treatment."),
]
